F484|THE CAPACITY QUESTION IS SETTLED BY CODE, NOT BY A PROBE - AND THE IDLE NODES ARE NOT AS IDLE AS OCCUPANCY SAYS|L289 said to read the code rather than write a third instrument, and reading it took two greps. THERE IS EXACTLY ONE TaskDispatched EMIT SITE IN THE SCHEDULER, AT LINE 779, AND EXACTLY ONE pick_device CALL, AT LINE 740, WHICH PRECEDES IT AND FILTERS d.in_flight LESS THAN d.cfg.weight. THERE IS NO SECOND DISPATCH PATH. The comment at line 550 about paths having DRIFTED APART is HISTORICAL - it describes six inline copies of an error-reading rule that were since extracted into one helper, not a live second dispatcher, and I had read it as evidence for a hypothesis it does not support. SO F481 IS DEFINITIVELY WRONG AND F482's DIRECTION WAS RIGHT: THE WEIGHT BINDS, and the three-concurrent-on-two-slots reading is an artefact of span reconstruction somewhere inside occupancy.py's concurrency histogram. I HAVE LOCALISED THAT DEFECT WITHOUT FIXING IT: closing attempts at task_retry was the obvious candidate, I applied it, AND IT CHANGED NO NUMBER, so the residual cause is elsewhere and I am not guessing at it tonight. THE FILE'S OWN GUARD NAMES THIS EXACT CONDITION - anything above the summed device weights IS IMPOSSIBLE AND INDICTS THIS SWEEP - AND IT FIRES WITHOUT BEING ENFORCED, WHICH IS A SECOND, SEPARATE DEFECT: A GUARD THAT REPORTS AN IMPOSSIBILITY AND CONTINUES IS DECORATION. SLOT UTILISATION STAYS WITHDRAWN. AND THE READING TURNED UP SOMETHING THAT MATTERS MORE THAN THE DISPUTE. Line 357 records that IDLE JOBS CLAIM in_flight - they bump a device's counter - BUT THEY EMIT NO task_dispatched, so occupancy.py cannot see them at all. occupancy.py already quantifies them under idle_slots, and the numbers are large. baseline-n3-r0: 3169 slot-seconds of idle-job work, 52.8 minutes, of which 1742s is JUDGE across 28 calls that each cost a slot. baseline-n3-r1: 2899 slot-seconds, 48.3 minutes, 1442s of judge over 20 calls. The 1-node cells show 1483s and 743