F490|THE IMPOSSIBLE READING IS GONE - A JUDGE KILL ENDS AN ATTEMPT AND NOTHING WAS CLOSING IT, SO SLOT UTILISATION IS RESTORED|F489 established that a judge kill emits only judge_verdict plus task_dispatched and never task_retry. Reading the kill path settles what it does: it ABORTS the worker future, removes claimed_device, DECREMENTS in_flight and releases held files, then re-queues the task as Ready. THE SLOT IS GENUINELY FREE FROM THAT INSTANT. So the device was never oversubscribed and the weight is enforced — but the task then WAITS IN THE READY QUEUE for a slot, and occupancy.py was closing its attempt at the NEXT DISPATCH, crediting that entire wait as busy. MEASURED ON baseline-n1-r1: test-api was judge-killed at 63.1 minutes and not re-dispatched until 76.7 — THIRTEEN POINT SIX MINUTES COUNTED AS RUNNING WHILE THE DEVICE WAS DEMONSTRABLY FREE. That one gap is the whole of the 816 seconds at three concurrent on a two-slot fleet. THE FIX IS ONE CANDIDATE ADDED TO THE ATTEMPT-END SET: an attempt now ends at its completion, the next dispatch, a split, a retry, OR A JUDGE VERDICT WHOSE ACTION IS re_dispatch OR fail. CONTROL PASSES IN BOTH DIRECTIONS AND IT IS THE FILE'S OWN GUARD DOING THE CHECKING: impossible_concurrency is now EMPTY ON ALL FIVE CELLS, where it previously fired [3] on baseline-n1-r1, and no cell's peak exceeds its slot_count. SLOT UTILISATION IS THEREFORE UN-WITHDRAWN, and these are the first numbers I am entitled to publish: 1-node 0.8642 and 0.9526; 3-node 0.6186, 0.5847 and 0.4323. STILL NO OVERLAP BETWEEN THE ARMS, and it says what device-busy could not — a 3-node fleet fills roughly HALF its slots while a 1-node fleet fills nearly all of them. ⚠️ NOTE HOW MUCH THE BAD SPANS MOVED: baseline-n3-r0's device occupancy was 0.5535 this morning and reads 0.7971 now, and baseline-n1-r1's slot utilisation went 0.9526 from an impossible 1.0047. EVERY OCCUPANCY FIGURE I QUOTED BEFORE THIS FIX ON A CELL WITH JUDGE KILLS WAS OVERSTATED, INCLUDING SOME