F494|THE CELL LANDED AT 0.4548 - THE WORST 3-NODE QUALITY EVER RECORDED - AND THE SCOREBOARD WAS QUIETLY LENDING ONE RUN'S CAPACITY NUMBERS TO ANOTHER|baseline-n3-r0 on a3fdfce02 finished: QUALITY 0.4548, SPEED 130 MINUTES, dev-busy 0.6893, slots 0.5083, 3-node pool, void false, aborted false, timed_out false, harness_ok true. Tiers A 0.833 over 6 checks, B 0.25 over 12, C 0.286 over 7, D 0.5 over 10 - the failure is concentrated in B and C, not spread. THAT IS THE LOWEST 3-NODE QUALITY ON THE BOARD BY A WIDE MARGIN, below the previous floor of 0.6904 and below BOTH 1-node cells, and it is also the second-slowest 3-node cell. Reporting the 21-of-21-at-94-minutes headline alone would have been a lie by omission: the tasks all completed and the app it produced is the worst one yet. TWO PILLARS, AND THIS CELL LOST BOTH. I WILL NOT ATTRIBUTE IT. n equals 1, this is the first cell on this commit, and it ran on a machine carrying eleven orphaned processes burning fifty CPU-minutes (F493) - a confound I discovered only after it finished. Note that the harness's own contended field reads 0 for this cell, which is not a measurement of contention but a field that cannot see orphans at all. THEN THE SCOREBOARD ITSELF TURNED OUT TO BE WRONG, and this is the part worth keeping. goal.py printed dev-busy 0.6893 and slots 0.5083 for BOTH the f9b6d2bd6 row and the a3fdfce02 row of baseline-n3-r0 - two runs 29 wall-minutes apart, sharing a cell name. capacity_of took a CELL NAME and called occupancy.analyse on the live directory, so an ARCHIVED result silently borrowed whatever log happened to be sitting there now. F487 recorded f9b6d2bd6's occupancy as 0.7971; the board replaced it with 0.6893 and nothing objected. Archiving the RESULT was only half a fix - the capacity numbers are computed from run.jsonl, and the sweep TRUNCATES that file when it reuses the directory, which it did at 21:32. SHIPPED: goal.py now snapshots every finished run.jsonl into _archive/logs keyed by the log's own mtime, and capacity_of takes a LOG PATH resolved to the newest snapshot at or before that row's result mtime. When no snapshot predates the row - which is exactly the f9b6d2bd6 case, because I only started snapshotting logs tonight - it prints SUPPRESSED (LOG GONE) instead of a neighbour's number. CONTROLLED: every row whose log survived prints EXACTLY the values it printed before the change, and only the one row with no surviving log changed, which is the behaviour a correct fix must have. ⚠️ I ALSO NEARLY PUBLISHED THAT THE RESULT ARCHIVE HAD FAILED. I listed _archive with a head -20, saw directories for three commits and no f9b6d2bd6, and concluded the 0.6904 cell was lost. The listing was TRUNCATED BY MY OWN PIPE and the f9b6d2bd6 archive was there all along, holding 0.6904 and 101 minutes exactly as written. That is the second self-inflicted read error in two turns and both were a truncating command, not a wrong idea. ⇒ L300 A ROW'S NUMBERS MUST COME FROM THAT ROW'S OWN EVIDENCE - IF AN IDENTIFIER RESOLVES THROUGH A MUTABLE DIRECTORY, THE OLDEST ROW ON THE BOARD IS ALWAYS SHOWING THE NEWEST RUN'S DATA
F495|THE FROZEN BINARY IS 071b57514 NOT 7abd351cf - I LABELLED THE FREEZE WITH THE WRONG COMMIT AND WOULD HAVE WAITED FOREVER FOR A PAIR THAT COULD NEVER FORM|F488 froze the binary at rebuild 9, identified as 1786119910-235858512 at commit 7abd351cf, and said hold until one 1-node and one 3-node cell land on it. THE FINGERPRINT WAS RIGHT AND THE COMMIT WAS WRONG. stat on target/release/goose reads mtime 1786119910 size 235858512, which is rebuild 9 exactly and UNCHANGED, so the freeze itself is intact and none of tonight's four commits are in it. But the build happened at 19:25:10, and HEAD at that moment was 071b57514, two commits past 7abd351cf. The intervening commits acfb96ead and 071b57514 are HARNESS ONLY - the F487 finding and goal.py archiving - so the binary contains exactly the six engine fixes intended, including the join-owns-nothing fix at 7abd351cf. THE SUBSTANCE IS CORRECT AND ONLY THE LABEL WAS WRONG, but the label is what I would have matched cells against: every cell built from this binary emits build_sha 071b57514, so a freeze pair defined as 7abd351cf would have matched ZERO cells and I would have kept waiting for a pair that could never form while the evidence accumulated under a name I was not looking at. The currently-running swarm-3node-r0 is the FIRST cell on 071b57514, 3-node, so the pair still needs a 1-NODE cell on 071b57514. ⚠️ AND THE FIRST HALF OF F493 IS VERIFIED: reap.py run immediately before this cell started found ZERO orphans, against eleven found after the previous one. The second half - a non-zero count after this cell whose leaders coincide with its retry or kill events - is still open. ⇒ L301 A BINARY'S FINGERPRINT AND ITS COMMIT ARE TWO DIFFERENT FACTS AND ONLY ONE OF THEM IS IN THE EVENT STREAM - RECORD THE ONE THE RUNS ACTUALLY EMIT, OR THE FREEZE MATCHES NOTHING
F496|THE SWEEP ALREADY HAD A REAPER AND I WROTE A SECOND ONE - THE REAL DEFECT WAS ITS THREE-HOUR FLOOR AGAINST A LEAK THE ENGINE MANUFACTURES EVERY 420 SECONDS|Before trusting reap.py I went looking for anything else that does the same job, and sweep.py line 1208 has reap_run_orphans, which is BETTER THAN MINE IN BOTH GUARDS THAT MATTER. It discriminates on the process's CWD being inside runs/, so nothing of Mihai's can ever match, where mine matched on COMMAND NAME and would have swept up any pytest anywhere. And it walks ppid to the sweep's root, so the live engine, its shells and their children are protected BY CONSTRUCTION however they are grouped - its own comment records that a group-only check left the running engine matching every condition with nothing but the age guard between it and SIGKILL. I had built a second implementation of one rule with a different discriminator and a different floor, which is the exact failure this campaign keeps rediscovering, and I built it without looking first. THE FIRST DRAFT STILL EARNED ITS KEEP, BECAUSE IT FOUND WHAT THE REAL REAPER WAS MISSING. Its floor is min_age_secs 10800 - THREE HOURS - applied uniformly. The two pytest orphans burning fifty CPU-minutes DURING the cell they were corrupting were 55 and 48 minutes old, so they were invisible to it, and their start times match that run's two agent stalled - no progress for 420s retries to the minute. THE ENGINE MANUFACTURES ONE OF THESE EVERY 420 SECONDS OF STALL AND THE REAPER WAS SET TO NOTICE THREE HOURS LATER. Widening the floor for everything would be wrong - the three hours was written for a different case, a stale process sharing a REUSED run directory path with a live cell - so the fix is a discriminator, not a number. PPID 1 IS WHAT THE AGE GUARD WAS STANDING IN FOR. A live worker's child has the engine as its parent; reparenting to init happens only once the parent is already dead, so a ppid-1 process under a run directory has NOBODY WAITING ON IT BY DEFINITION rather than by inference from its age. Shipped: orphan_age_secs, default 600, applied only to ppid-1 processes; everything else keeps the three-hour floor untouched. The ancestry walk is unaffected because a process descending from the sweep never has ppid 1. Also shipped: a dry_run parameter, so the manual entry point can report before it signals. reap.py is now a THIN CLI over that one function and implements nothing. CONTROLLED IN BOTH DIRECTIONS ON THE SAME OBJECT, which is the check a zero from any reaper requires: a detached sleep started with cwd under runs/ is SEEN at floor 0 and SPARED at the 600s default one second later, and across both passes the live sweep pid 41030 and the live engine pid 4926 were never listed and are still running. ⚠️ THE RUNNING SWEEP WILL NOT SEE THIS EDIT - it has been up for a day and is executing the old function from memory (L265), so until it restarts the manual script is the only path to the new floor. PREDICTION, WITH ITS INERT CASE: on the next cell that records an agent stalled retry, reap.py run immediately after it finishes will list a ppid-1 orphan aged between 10 minutes and 3 hours - one the old floor would have missed. INERT IF that cell records no stall retry. INERT IS NOT A PASS. ⇒ L302 BEFORE WRITING A GUARD, GREP FOR THE ONE THAT ALREADY EXISTS - THE USEFUL FINDING IS ALMOST NEVER THAT THE RULE IS ABSENT, IT IS THAT THE RULE IS CALIBRATED FOR A CASE THAT STOPPED BEING THE COMMON ONE
F497|THE CEILING IS THE PLAN, NOT THE FLEET - THE DAG CAN USE 2.2 TO 3.8 NODES AND WE ARE GIVING IT SIX SLOTS, AND occupancy.py HAD ALREADY COMPUTED THIS AND I NEVER READ THE FIELD|Looking for the Amdahl bound on Mihai's question I was about to write a probe for it, applied L302 and grepped first, and occupancy.py has exported max_useful_nodes, critical_path_secs and solo_share_of_wall the entire time. max_useful_nodes is total_work divided by critical_path - the node count beyond which a plan CANNOT go faster whatever the fleet - and the file's own comment already states the consequence: if that number is below the pool size, the answer to does the swarm get better with more nodes is decided by the PLANNER, not the scheduler, and the work belongs in the architect prompt. THE NUMBERS, ACROSS ALL SIX ARCHIVED CELLS: max_useful_nodes reads 2.15, 2.23, 2.65, 3.55, 3.79 and 3.79. NOT SIX. The 3-node fleet is 3 devices at weight 2 = SIX SLOTS, and the DAG can use between two and four of them. The critical path is 57 to 73 percent of wall on five of the six cells, so even an INFINITE fleet could only ever cut a run by about a third. I TRUSTED THE NUMBER ONLY AFTER CHECKING WHAT COULD INFLATE IT, because a decisive figure that confirms a story is when to be most suspicious. dag_unknown_rooted is ZERO on all six - the flattering default that once read 3.0 where the truth was 1.5 never fired here. phantom_tail_tasks is zero everywhere. Only baseline-n3-r3 is compromised, with 5 split children and 2 unfinished tasks and an outlier critical-path ratio of 0.30, so I am flagging that row rather than leaning on it. THEN AN INDEPENDENT CROSS-CHECK LANDED THAT I DID NOT GO LOOKING FOR. If the plan is the binding constraint then slot utilisation should equal max_useful_nodes divided by slot count, and it does: n3-r1 predicts 0.592 against a measured 0.5847, n3-r2 predicts 0.442 against a measured 0.4323. Two computations from different parts of the log, agreeing to within a percent.