F509|I FIRED THE PRE-DECIDED REAP TWO MINUTES LATE, SO I TURNED THE RULE INTO A GUARD - AND THE OBVIOUS SIMPLIFICATION WOULD HAVE BEEN UNSAFE|F506 decided, before the outcome was known, that the reap must fire the instant run_finished appears and BEFORE baseline-n1-r0 starts. The sweep log says the 1-node cell started at 22:49:57 and my reap ran around 22:52. TWO MINUTES LATE. Harmless in fact - the new cell was still in research at four events, so nothing had bound a port - but the rule was not executed as written and the reason is structural, not carelessness: MY LANDING SIGNAL WAS THE SCRATCH DIRECTORY BEING RESET, AND THAT ONLY HAPPENS WHEN THE NEXT UNIT BEGINS. The trigger I chose could not fire before the thing it was supposed to precede. A rule whose trigger is inherently late is not a rule I can keep by trying harder. THE TEMPTING SIMPLIFICATION IS WRONG AND I ALMOST SHIPPED IT. Having established in F502 that a live worker's child stays parented to the engine for the whole run, the natural conclusion is that ppid-1 ALREADY encodes within-cell versus cross-cell, making --kill safe at any moment and the timing rule unnecessary. IT DOES NOT. A worker that launches cmd ampersand - the exact pattern the sweep's own reaper docstring quotes verbatim from a real leak - ORPHANS ITS CHILD IMMEDIATELY, while its own cell is still running. So a live cell really can produce ppid-1 processes under runs/, and a ten-minute age floor would eventually reap one that belongs to the run being measured. I caught this by asking what would have to be true rather than by testing it, which is the cheaper order. THE CORRECT TEST IS PROVENANCE, NOT AGE, AND IT IS THE SAME SHAPE AS F504's: a process that STARTED BEFORE THE CURRENTLY-LIVE ENGINE CANNOT BELONG TO IT. Shipped: reap.py --kill now finds any live goose swarm run, takes its age, and REFUSES with exit 3 if any target is younger, naming them. CONTROLLED IN BOTH DIRECTIONS: with an empty target list and a live 12.1-minute engine