F570|I READ THE ARTEFACT AND FOUND THE 0.222 - ONE TYPE ERROR IN ONE LINE KILLS NINE OF TWELVE BEHAVIOURAL CHECKS ON AN APP THE SMOKE GATE CANNOT FAULT|F569 concluded that the tier-B lever is invisible to every counter and that only reading or running the artefact would ever see it. So I read the artefact. baseline-n1-r2 scored tier B 0.222 and its code is, structurally, fine: store.all_payments orders by utc_ts, upsert_many counts new ids correctly, the pagination handler defaults to 25, caps at 100, slices by offset and reports the true total, and `python3 -m vendorsync --help` exits 0 with the exact --db and --port the spec advertises. Every structural thing I checked was RIGHT, which is precisely why tier A is 0.833-ish and tier B is 0.222 — F556's right-shape-wrong-substance, in one file. THE FIRST DEFECT IS ONE LINE. api.py line 62 reads `self.rfile.read(self.headers.get("Content-Length", 0))`. `headers.get` returns a STRING when the header is present and the integer 0 only when it is absent, and `read` rejects a string. I proved it directly: parsing a real HTTPMessage gives '5', and `read('5')` raises TypeError, argument should be integer or None. The handler wraps everything in a bare `except Exception` that returns 500. THE SCORER'S `_post` SENDS `data=b""`, AND urllib SETS `Content-Length: 0` WHENEVER data IS NOT NONE — I confirmed the header on the real Request object. So the app receives the STRING '0', raises, and answers 500 TO EVERY SINGLE /api/sync. Nothing is ever synced. sync_completeness scores 0, resync_idempotent reports second sync failed, the store stays empty so payment_row_shape has no rows, total_field is wrong, chronological_order and local_pagination are vacuous, and both summary checks collapse. NINE OR TEN OF TWELVE TIER-B CHECKS DIE FROM ONE MISSING int(). THE SECOND DEFECT IS A PATH. The spec names `vendorsync/web/index.html`; the app wrote `./web/index.html` at the repo root instead. That costs modules_present a fifth of its score and takes serves_page with it, and the three UI behavioural checks — ui_states, ui_currency, ui_offline — all read the served page, so all three score zero. Together the two defects account for essentially the whole 0.222. AND THIS IS EXACTLY WHY THE ENGINE'S GATE CANNOT SEE IT. run_smoke_gate checks that pytest collects, that the app's OWN generated tests pass, that `--help` exits 0, and that advertised commands exiting zero do not print errors. This app satisfies every one of those. Its own tests never POST to /api/sync with a Content-Length header, so they never hit the line. F561 measured the gate as a WORKING PROXY that is NECESSARY BUT NOT SUFFICIENT, and this is what the insufficiency looks like in source: a perfectly-shaped, importable, help-able, self-test-passing application that answers 500 to the only request that matters. ⚠️ AND I NEARLY PUBLISHED A DIFFERENT, WRONG CAUSE. I saw `MeridianClient("http://127.0.0.1:8946", ...)` hardcoded in __main__.py, and since the vendor port is randomised per run I was ready to call it a build-time-versus-score-time mismatch. Reading run_build.py killed it: `server = vendor_service.serve(port, trace)` wraps BOTH `invoke` and `score_build.gather` in one try block, so the vendor is on the same port for both. SEVENTH source-shaped claim to die on measurement today, caught before publishing. ⚠️ n IS ONE APP. This names why THIS cell scored 0.222; it does not establish that a stray type error is the general shape of tier-B loss, and the 0.917 cell's tree was overwritten so I cannot contrast them. ⇒ L376 WHEN THE COUNTERS ARE EXHAUSTED, OPEN THE FILE - THE DIFFERENCE BETWEEN 0.917 AND 0.222 WAS A MISSING int() AND A MISPLACED HTML FILE, AND NO EVENT, DIGEST OR CORRELATION IN THIS ENTIRE CAMPAIGN COULD HAVE POINTED AT EITHER
F571|THE 15-OF-15 ABSTENTION IS A DELIBERATE DISCLOSURE THAT `POST /api/sync` WAS NEVER PROBED - AND THE ENGINE HAD ALREADY FOUND F570 BEFORE I DID, IN THE SAME WORDS|F564 attributed spec_contract's inconclusive-on-every-cell to the already-bound-port guard. F567 measured the spec, proved that attribution impossible, WITHDREW it, and named two remaining candidates: the spawn failing, or the server never coming up. BOTH ARE WRONG. The real reason is a third thing neither attribution considered, and it is sitting at swarm.rs:17481 under the heading NO SILENT CAPS: after probing, the check calls `spec_unprobed_advertised(spec)` and pushes an inconclusive naming every advertised endpoint it did NOT probe, with the explicit rationale that not probing an endpoint is an admission about the CHECK, not a defect in the app. On this bed that list is exactly one entry — POST /api/sync — which is why `inconclusive` is 1 in fifteen of fifteen cells and why `verified` is the constant 2 that F568 found carries zero information: the spec advertises two probeable GETs and one POST, every single run. THREE OF MY ATTRIBUTIONS FOR ONE NUMBER, AND THE ANSWER WAS A FUNCTION CALL I HAD NOT READ. AND THE ENGINE HAD ALREADY FOUND F570, BEFORE I DID, WORD FOR WORD. `spec_unprobed_advertised`'s doc comment reads: the table holds GET /api/health, GET /api/payments, GET /api/summary and POST /api/sync — AND THE POST IS EXACTLY THE ENDPOINT WHOSE DOCUMENTED RESPONSE SHAPE FOUR OF FIVE ARCHIVED CELLS GET WRONG. THE GATE THAT EXISTS TO CLOSE FALSE GREENS WAS BLIND TO THE ONE ENDPOINT THAT WAS ACTUALLY BROKEN. That is F570's conclusion, measured across five cells, written in the source before I opened a single app file. What F570 adds is the MECHANISM the comment does not have: the response shape is wrong because `self.rfile.read(self.headers.get("Content-Length", 0))` passes a STRING to `read`, the bare except turns the TypeError into a 500, and the endpoint answers 500 to every request. The engine